<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Technology – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Tech-Free or Tech Bound?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of extended instructions on familiar topics. Interactive: Interpret peer feedback in collaborative projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow extended instructions on tech-free routines by organizing a sequence of steps during group work (e.g., "Turn off your phone after school, then do a puzzle, read a chapter from a book, and write in your journal."). Can respond to instructions on how to reduce screen time (e.g., "What should we do?" "Read a book instead."). Can show comprehension of peer feedback by interpreting key points about technology use (e.g., "What did Anna say about her tech-free day?" "She read a lot.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, and introducing core vocabulary and pronunciation in preparation for the lesson's listening focus.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to close their eyes and think about the last time they used a <i>device</i> (e.g., cellphone, tablet, computer). After 10 seconds, students open their eyes and quickly share one word that describes their action or feeling during that time. (e.g., "game," "chat," "bored," "fun").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the new core vocabulary: <i>technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, reduce, disconnect, benefits, distracted, focused</i>. The teacher says each word clearly, models its pronunciation, and shows a flashcard or image for each. For instance, holding up a phone and saying "This is a <i>device</i>, a <i>cellphone</i>. We use <i>technology</i>." The teacher specifically emphasizes clear articulation of these initial vocabulary words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary.</p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a book a <i>device</i>?" (Expected answer: "No, a book is not a device.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you turn off your Wi-Fi, do you <i>disconnect</i> from the <i>internet</i>?" (Expected answer: "Yes, you disconnect from the internet.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are exposed to new language through an authentic audio input, focusing on general and specific comprehension related to the scenario "Technology."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio recording (1.5-2 minutes) of two teenagers, Maria and Juan, discussing a new "Tech-Free Challenge" at school. The audio includes instructions for participating and opinions on its <i>benefits</i>. It integrates modals ("You <i>can</i> read a book," "We <i>should</i> reduce screen time") and new vocabulary (e.g., "<i>social media</i>," "<i>distracted</i>," "<i>focused</i>").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret the main idea and key details in short, simple audio messages and instructions related to personal routines and common activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listening):</b> The teacher asks, "What are Maria and Juan talking about?" (Expected answer: "A tech-free challenge at school.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listening):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements are True or False.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Maria thinks the challenge will make her more <i>distracted</i>." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Juan suggests they <i>disconnect</i> from <i>social media</i> after school." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One of the <i>benefits</i> of the challenge is more time for hobbies." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third Listening, if needed):</b> Students listen for specific suggestions and match them to potential tech-free activities mentioned (e.g., "Read a book," "Spend time with friends," "Do a puzzle").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on building accuracy in listening and responding, utilizing the target grammar and vocabulary from the lesson through structured activities.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension for instructions and suggestions. Grammar: Modals ("can," "could," "should") for ability and suggestions. Vocabulary: <i>technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused</i>. Pragmatic: Giving opinions ("I think technology is useful..."), Offering suggestions ("You could try...").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher plays short sentences or phrases related to tech use and tech-free activities. Students listen and fill in the blanks in a handout with the correct modal verb (can, could, should) or vocabulary word. For example, "You _______ read a book instead of watching TV." (should); "It's _______ to talk to friends in person than online." (easier).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Transform:</b> The teacher reads a statement. Students listen and transform it based on a given cue, focusing on understanding the change. For example:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "We *use* our cellphones too much." Student A listens, then responds: "We *should reduce* cellphone use."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "It's hard to stay *focused* with all the notifications." Student B listens, then responds: "It's *easier* to stay focused if you *disconnect*."</li>
                    </ul>
                    The teacher monitors pronunciation, especially the /θ/ and /ð/ sounds where relevant (e.g., "with," "than," "the").
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: Listening for Suggestions:</b> In pairs, Student A describes a common problem with technology (e.g., "I spend too much time on *social media* and feel <i>distracted</i>."). Student B listens carefully and offers a suggestion using a modal verb, integrating new vocabulary (e.g., "You <i>could try</i> to <i>disconnect</i> for an hour each day."). Then they switch roles. Students listen for key points in their partner's problem and suggestion.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students engage in a collaborative task where their listening comprehension is paramount for understanding instructions and peer contributions in a simulated scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Work - "Tech-Free Routine Planner".</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides a scenario: "Your school is promoting a 'Tech-Free Evening' once a week. You need to create a sequence of activities for this evening."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group member takes turns orally suggesting one tech-free activity and explains its <i>benefits</i> (e.g., "I think we <i>should</i> read a book because it helps us stay <i>focused</i>.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other group members must <b>listen carefully</b> to all suggestions. After hearing everyone's ideas, they collectively decide on a final sequence of 3-4 steps for their "Tech-Free Evening." They might use comparatives to discuss which activity is "more relaxing" or "easier to do."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student in each group acts as a "listener-recorder," whose role is to actively listen to all ideas and note down the agreed-upon sequence of instructions/activities.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, the recorder shares the group's sequence aloud with the class, demonstrating that they successfully followed and interpreted the group's collective "instructions" and feedback. (e.g., "First, we <i>should</i> <i>disconnect</i> all <i>devices</i>. Then, we <i>can</i> play board games. After that, we <i>could</i> read a chapter from a book, and finally, we <i>should</i> talk about our day.").</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in offering suggestions and opinions, active listening to understand peer contributions and complex instructions, and collaborative task completion by organizing a sequence of steps.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students' listening comprehension is formatively assessed through observation during a task, followed by individual reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "Tech-Free Routine Planner" activity, the teacher uses an observation checklist to note individual students' ability to:</p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follow extended instructions (e.g., understanding the task of creating a sequence).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpret peer feedback and suggestions (e.g., acknowledging and integrating group members' ideas).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond appropriately to questions or suggestions from peers.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Accurately follows multi-step instructions and interprets main points of peer contributions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates new vocabulary (e.g., <i>reduce, disconnect, benefits, focused</i>) when responding or summarizing.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Response:</b> Provides intelligible and relevant responses to peer feedback/suggestions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in group discussion, showing an understanding of others' input.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts individually:</p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new suggestion you heard today to help <i>reduce</i> screen time?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to follow the instructions during the group activity? Why?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage allows for student and teacher reflection on the lesson, informing future instructional decisions and connecting to the subsequent lesson in the sequence.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped you listen better to English instructions?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has this lesson changed your thoughts about being 'Tech-Free or Tech Bound'?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of engaging students and developing listening skills with the new vocabulary and grammar? The audio input seemed appropriate for the CEFR level. Students successfully used modals for suggestions. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with extended instructions or identifying key details? Perhaps providing a visual aid or a simple flowchart for sequencing steps in the next listening activity. We need more practice with distinguishing specific word sounds, possibly through a brief warm-up focused on /θ/ and /ð/ minimal pairs in the next lesson if speaking activities allow.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short article or blog post about "Tech-Free Challenges" or "<i>Benefits</i> of <i>Reducing</i> Screen Time," using the vocabulary introduced today to identify details and understand context from a written text.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____ / ____]</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>